00:02:25.074 --> 00:02:28.914
California's coasts spent the week under hazard warnings as low pressure systems drove

00:02:28.914 --> 00:02:31.454
in the biggest waves in years, the Guardian reports.

00:02:32.174 --> 00:02:33.854
Beaches and piers closed last weekend.

00:02:34.434 --> 00:02:38.774
Offshore buoys run by the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration logged waves

00:02:38.774 --> 00:02:41.294
above 30 feet, 9 meters last Friday.

00:02:41.294 --> 00:02:47.374
The Monterey Bowie hit 34.1 feet, a mark the Guardian says would be the highest there in 30 years.

00:02:47.854 --> 00:02:49.214
Alerts run through Friday night.

00:02:49.714 --> 00:02:56.814
San Luis Obispo County beaches and Santa Barbara's Central Coast are forecast at 10 to 18 feet with sets of 18 to 22.

00:02:57.314 --> 00:03:02.694
The same Pacific storms are crossing the Western United States with heavy rain, mountain snow, and strong wind.

00:03:03.274 --> 00:03:06.454
San Francisco had what the Guardian calls record rainfall on Wednesday.

00:03:06.454 --> 00:03:12.414
In Sacramento, 32,000 customers lost power. By Sunday night, a further low is expected

00:03:12.414 --> 00:03:17.554
to drop more than 100 millimeters, 4 inches, in places across Southern Oregon and Northern

00:03:17.554 --> 00:03:23.193
California, with more snow at Donner Pass in the Northern Sierra. Dominion Dynamics has

00:03:23.193 --> 00:03:29.353
